  "notes_context": "These notes were distilled from earlier work in this repository that passed verification. Follow them unless the code or CHANGELOG.md shows they no longer hold.\n- [at-0001] Round money only with round_money: Always round monetary results with ledgerly.money.round_money (half-up to the cent); never use Python's round() or a bare Decimal.quantize(), which use banker's rounding and produce wrong invoice totals.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0002] Convert amounts with to_money and ZERO: Convert raw amounts to Decimal via ledgerly.money.to_money and seed sums with ledgerly.money.ZERO; to_money rejects float and bool inputs with a BillingError.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0003] Raise BillingError with stable snake_case codes: Signal domain failures by raising `BillingError(code, detail)` from `ledgerly.errors` with stable snake_case codes such as `invalid_state`, `invalid_quantity` and `negative_amount`; reuse `invalid_state` with the message `f\"invoice {invoice.id} is {invoice.status}\"` for non-draft invoices.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0004] Reject bools when validating integer fields: When validating an integer argument, exclude `bool` explicitly (`isinstance(quantity, bool) or not isinstance(quantity, int)`) because `True`/`False` are ints in Python and would otherwise pass a positive-integer check.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)",
